  "preamble": "Lowering and unrounding of [o] to [a] is one of the defining mergers of several Indo-European branches: PIE short *o fell together with *a in Germanic, Indo-Iranian and Albanian (PIE *nókʷts 'night' > Gothic nahts, Albanian natë; PIE *pótis > Sanskrit páti-). East Slavic akanye turns unstressed /o/ into [a] (Belarusian вада [vaˈda] 'water'), and Proto-Northern-Iroquoian independently lowered Proto-Iroquoian *o to *a. In the Arawakan language Mojeño, Proto-Mojeño *o merged with *a in the Ignaciano dialect, while Trinitario keeps o.",
